@page "/status-dot"

<CatHead Title="Status dots">
    The state of something a view is connected to &mdash; a device, a stream, a job runner, an API
    &mdash; as a dot and a word, small enough for a topbar, a table cell or a card header. A state
    of a record rather than a connection is a <a href="/badge">badge</a>; whether a person is
    available is an <a href="/avatar#presence-mark">avatar's presence mark</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.StatusDot.Connection" Title="Connection state" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.health-badge</code> with a <code>.health-dot</code>, in six states: the active
        <code>--live</code>, <code>--streaming</code> and <code>--connecting</code> pulse, and the
        settled <code>--idle</code>, <code>--degraded</code> and <code>--down</code> do not.
        <strong>The word is not optional</strong> &mdash; a colour alone fails for anybody who
        cannot see it. <code>.stale</code> is how old the number beside it is, with
        <code>--warn</code> when it is old enough to distrust. The circuit's own state is the
        <a href="/status-bar">status bar</a>'s job, not a dot's.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.StatusDot.InATable" Title="In a table" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One feed per row, its state in a column of its own and a <code>.stale</code> beside the
        figure it dates. A page whose services are partly down keeps working around them &mdash;
        see <a href="/live-state#degraded-page">a degraded page</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>
